    "objid": "0001",
    "raw": "MOTIVATION (incident 2026-08-06, repo opportunity, todo d56d5d65): a worker session ran work-item-read, worked for hours, then work-item-done -- but a driver session had groomed the same todo in between (add-subtodo consumed the cursor as start_subtodo; inserts added a barrier). Because work-item-done addresses its target POSITIONALLY ('the first not-done item'), the worker's commit silently bound to the wrong item (the barrier). Store-level write locking held fine; the record stayed well-formed; the semantic BINDING was wrong and needed hand repair. The conflicting unit is a work session (minutes-hours), not a CLI invocation.\n\nDECISIONS (ratified 2026-08-06):\n- Command chaining with '+' as separator: REJECTED. It locks for seconds; the race spans a session. Also argv quoting is a minefield ('+' occurs inside --summary text). If scripted atomicity is ever needed, a batch subcommand reading JSON is the shape -- deferred, no current need.\n- Standalone lock/unlock commands: REJECTED as a separate namespace. The schema already reserves working {owner, expire} for multi-owner handoff; a lock that IS the working state cannot drift from it. Timeout and --force live there as lease semantics.\n- Single-owner doctrine stands (agent == actor). The mechanisms below make violations LOUD and recoverable, not impossible.\n\nLAYER 1 -- item identity + stale-write detection (CAS). The foundation; fixes the observed bug with no locks. Every WorkItem carries a stable id (record migration backfills missing ones; schema version bump). work-item-read returns the cursor item's id + a record revision (update_dt or counter). Cursor-mutating commands (work-item-done, work-item-insert/replace/delete, add-subtodo) accept --item <id> and --expect-rev <rev> and FAIL LOUDLY on mismatch, naming what moved and who moved it. work-item-read's next hint embeds id+rev, so the standard poll loop is protected with zero agent behavior change. Works across arbitrary gaps, survives crashes, no timeout tuning.\n\nLAYER 2 -- activate the working lease (advisory ownership, session scope). set --state working --owner <who> stamps expire (default ~4h); any write by the owner refreshes it. Enforcement is SCOPED TO THE CURSOR AND DONE-PREFIX: work-item-done/insert/replace/delete and add-subtodo from a non-owner fail (naming the holder) unless the lease is expired or --force is given. work-item-add (appending future items), Body edits, and tags stay free -- the HICAP-driver-grooms / worker-implements overlap is legitimate. doctor sweeps expired leases (the existing 'unlocked' counter is the natural home).\n\nLAYER 3 -- per-invocation multi-record transactions (internal, invisible). Each invocation computes its touched-todo set (parent+child for add-subtodo/merge-subtodo/wait-and-merge) and wraps ALL writes in one store transaction: sqlite BEGIN IMMEDIATE; a lockfile for the json-dir backend. No user-facing syntax.\n\nORDERING: Layer 1 first (prevents the incident class outright), then 2, then 3. Each layer lands with tests; the incident scenario is the canonical regression test. Update the todos SKILL.md (living document) as each layer lands.\n\nTIERS: implementation MIDCAP; verification LOCAP; escalate discovered ambiguity to the user."
  },
